WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.992
Mutual TLS, often shortened to mTLS, is a pattern where both sides of a
connection may present certificates.

2
00:00:05.992 --> 00:00:11.325
The client validates the server certificate, and the server validates the
client certificate.

3
00:00:11.325 --> 00:00:22.980
This can strengthen service identity for service-to-service communication,
device identity, workload identity, controlled partner integrations, or
other environments where both endpoints are managed.

4
00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.931
Client certificate validation requires its own trust configuration.

5
00:00:26.931 --> 00:00:32.133
The server needs to know which client certificate authorities or trust
anchors are approved.

6
00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:37.795
The application also needs to map certificate information to an
application identity at a high level.

7
00:00:37.795 --> 00:00:46.026
A certificate may establish that a workload, device, or service is known,
but the application still has to decide what that identity is allowed to
do.

8
00:00:46.026 --> 00:00:48.989
mTLS does not automatically authorize every action.

9
00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:55.245
A known service should not necessarily access every endpoint, tenant,
operation, or administrative function.

10
00:00:55.245 --> 00:00:58.734
Least privilege still applies after identity is established.

11
00:00:58.734 --> 00:01:04.331
Application policy must enforce permissions, resource access, business
rules, and auditability.

12
00:01:04.331 --> 00:01:09.796
Transport identity can be a strong signal, but it is not a complete
authorization system by itself.

13
00:01:09.796 --> 00:01:13.023
Operational lifecycle matters for client certificates.

14
00:01:13.023 --> 00:01:22.636
Certificates expire, keys need protection, client identities are
provisioned and deprovisioned, trust stores change, and incidents may
require rotation or revocation.

15
00:01:22.636 --> 00:01:34.752
Logs can record safe client-certificate events such as identity category,
issuer, validation outcome, and correlation identifiers, but they should
avoid private keys, secrets, or excessive certificate material.

16
00:01:34.752 --> 00:01:37.320
mTLS is powerful when operated deliberately.
